      overview: "Each Sukuk category carries its own structuring rules, tradability conditions and real-world examples, from Mudarabah certificates to the IDB's mixed portfolio Solidarity Trust Sukuk.",
      simpleExplanation: "Different Sukuk are built on different underlying contracts - a Mudarabah Sukuk represents a share in a profit-sharing venture, an Ijarah Sukuk represents part-ownership of a leased asset earning rent, a Salam or Istisna'a Sukuk represents a claim tied to a future delivery or manufactured item, and a mixed portfolio Sukuk (like the IDB's) blends several of these together to diversify risk and return.",
      academicExplanation: "Mudarabah/Muqaradah Sukuk represent common ownership entitling holders to a share in specific projects/activities managed on the Mudarabah principle, with the issuer as Mudarib and subscribers as capital owners; per the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council (1988): MS represent common ownership and entitle holders to a share in the specific project; the prospectus/contract must disclose all Shari'ah-required Qirad information (capital nature, profit ratio, conditions); MS holders may sell in the securities market, with sale rules depending on whether Mudarabah capital is still money (Bai' al Sarf rules apply), debt (debt-trading rules apply), or a mixed combination (market-price-by-consent); the manager/SPV may invest own funds and earn a separate capital-contribution profit in addition to Mudarib's share; neither the prospectus nor MS may guarantee capital or a fixed/percentage-based profit; contingency reserves may be created by deducting a percentage from profit each period; a totally unrelated third party may promise to donate to cover losses (but the issuer itself cannot guarantee Mudarabah capital). Musharakah Sukuk represent proportionate ownership in a partnership project/asset, issued as redeemable certificates for corporate or individual purposes (rehabilitation, vehicle purchase, clinics, factories, etc.); rules mirror Mudarabah Sukuk, with the key difference that the intermediary is a partner among certificate holders (like a joint stock company); tradable in the secondary market once the project starts, subject to the portfolio comprising nonliquid assets worth more than 50%; profit shared per agreed ratio, loss shared pro rata; Musharakah is considered more equitable/safer than Mudarabah since it involves both profit- and loss-sharing by the fund manager. Sudan has issued central bank Musharakah certificates (CMCs) and government Musharakah certificates (GMCs) since 1998, used in place of treasury bills for open market operations. Ijarah Sukuk mobilize funds for long-term infrastructure (airports, roads, schools, hospitals) by securitizing tangible assets; a lessor recovering purchase cost can sell the leased asset (wholly/partly) evidenced by certificates representing pro rata undivided ownership with all related rights/obligations - the rental being the return. Types include Sukuk of ownership in leased assets, and Sukuk of ownership of usufruct (of existing assets - tradable before sub-leasing, becoming a debt certificate once sub-leased; of future assets per description - tradable only after the asset is identified; of services of a specified supplier; of future services per description). Key features: the leased asset and rent amount must be clearly known; the lessor can sell the leased asset without hindering the lessee's benefit (new owners receive remaining rentals); the Ijarah certificate holder assumes ownership rights/obligations pro rata, bearing loss on total asset destruction; expenses related to the corpus are the owner's responsibility (not the lessee's), making returns quasi-fixed, not absolutely fixed; the SPV manages the process (purchase asset, issue Sukuk, lease to government/corporate body, distribute rentals); Ijarah certificates can be freely traded once ownership has transferred to Sukuk holders, and issuers may redeem before maturity at market or agreed price. Salam Sukuk are certificates for mobilizing advance-payment capital for later commodity delivery; the seller of the Salam commodity issues the certificates, subscribers are the buyers; a Parallel Salam structure entitles Sukuk holders to the commodity or its selling price at delivery. Salam sale benefits the seller (advance cash flow) and buyer (Salam price typically below spot price); BMA developed LIBOR-related, three-month Salam-based aluminium securities in 2001 with Bahrain Islamic Bank. Secondary-market trading of Salam Sukuk is generally considered impermissible since certificates represent a share in Salam debt, subject to debt-trading rules - though this needs further analysis regarding reselling goods before the original buyer's possession. Istisna'a Sukuk document the deferred price payable in an Istisna'a manufacturing/construction contract; the issuer is the manufacturer/seller, subscribers/holders are the buyers of the item to be produced; trading/redemption is permissible if funds have converted into owned assets during Istisna'a operation, but if realized funds are immediately paid in a Parallel Istisna'a or the item is submitted to the ultimate purchaser, trading follows debt-disposal rules (since the Sukuk then represents a monetary debt), meaning such certificates can only be cashed at maturity/face value, not sold at a discount to a third party (though transferable at face value). Murabaha securitization/Sukuk: Murabaha receivables (a note evidencing indebtedness) cannot be securitized into negotiable Sukuk for secondary trading - transfer must be at par value under Hawalah rules; but if a commodity is purchased but not yet sold, certificates against it are tradable as they represent a tradable asset. A mixed portfolio with Murabaha, if the pool's nonliquid/fixed (e.g. Ijarah) assets exceed 50% of total worth (majority view) or nonliquid assets exceed 10% (Hanafi view), can issue negotiable certificates. Arcapita Bank (Bahrain) issued five-year multicurrency Murabaha-backed Sukuk in 2005 (three-month LIBOR + 175bps, full recourse to Arcapita). Mixed Portfolio Sukuk combine Musharakah, Ijarah, and some Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a and Ju'alah contracts, with risk/return depending on the chosen contract mix; the prominent example is IDB's $400 million Solidarity Trust Sukuk (2003): Solidarity Trust Services (STS) as trustee issued fixed-rate trust certificates purchasing a portfolio of Ijarah, Murabaha and Istisna'a contracts originated by IDB, each certificate ranking pari passu; over 50% of assets had to be Ijarah at all times, with a dissolution event (triggering IDB's obligation to purchase all trust assets per a 'purchase undertaking deed') if Ijarah assets fell below 25%; profit net of trust expenses funded periodic returns; certificates redeemed at 100% of principal value; IDB separately guaranteed payment amounts scheduled by obligors (not a direct guarantee of trust certificate payments) and provided an interest-free facility to STS to ensure timely distributions.",
      examExplanation: "Mudarabah Sukuk sale rules depend on capital form: still cash -> Bai' al Sarf rules; debt form -> debt-trading rules; mixed -> market price by consent. Musharakah/Mudarabah Sukuk tradable once project starts if nonliquid assets >50%. Ijarah Sukuk = quasi-fixed (corpus expenses stay with owner). Salam Sukuk = not tradable except at face value (debt-trading rules). Istisna'a Sukuk = tradable only if funds converted to owned assets; otherwise face-value-only debt certificate. Murabaha receivables alone cannot be securitized for trading; mixed pool with >50% nonliquid assets can. IDB Solidarity Trust Sukuk: minimum 50% Ijarah assets, dissolution trigger below 25% Ijarah, IDB purchase undertaking + interest-free facility (not a direct guarantee of trust certificates).",
