  chapterSummary: "Chapter 15 explains how securitization converts illiquid Islamic-bank and corporate/sovereign assets into tradable Sukuk certificates through a Special Purpose Vehicle, details the AAOIFI-recognized Sukuk categories (Mudarabah, Musharakah, Ijarah, Salam, Istisna'a, Murabaha and mixed-portfolio), and sets out the Shari'ah rules governing which Sukuk are tradable in the secondary market. It also candidly flags the industry's most serious unresolved problems: widespread use of Bai' al 'Inah and Bai' al Dayn in some markets, near-universal marketing of Sukuk as fixed-return instruments despite the quasi-fixed nature Shari'ah actually permits, complex interdependent contract structures, and over-reliance on Ijarah Sukuk at the expense of Shirkah-based alternatives. Real-world case studies (Nakheel, Pakistan's sovereign Sukuk, WAPDA, Hanco) illustrate how these principles are applied in practice.",
